## 1. The standard

- National Core Arts Standards, 2014 (National Coalition for Core Arts Standards), the performance standards.
- Loaded in data/standards/ncas-performance-standards.txt as ids only, no wording: 1,264 ids across the five
  disciplines (visual arts 195, music 264, theater 298, dance 273, media arts 234), from the 2021-11 "at a glance"
  handouts, fetched 2026-10-27, and the 2021-11 music composition and theory strand handout, downloaded and read
  2026-11-21 (Cory, Desk q-fix-downloads-1). Format `DIS:<process><anchor>.<standard>.<grade><letter>`, for example
  `DA:Cr1.1.2a`. High school levels are `.Ia`, `.IIa`, `.IIIa` (media arts cells with one statement carry no
  letter, as `MA:Re9.1.I`).
- Eleven anchor standards in four artistic processes: creating (Cr1 to Cr3), performing, presenting or producing
  (Pr4 to Pr6), responding (Re7 to Re9), connecting (Cn10, Cn11). The bar asks for all four processes in every
  course (criterion 1).
- scripts/standards.py checks NCAS codes by a closed pattern on discipline, process and anchor, and accepts the
  strand letter and the grade or level (read 2026-11-19), and checks every NCAS id against the id file (data/plans/
  later.csv L83, done 2026-11-21). Each scope and sequence still states its id count against the table in section
  3.2, and a reader checks that each id's meaning is taught, which the script cannot see.
- Music is loaded for pre-kindergarten to grade 8 general music and for the high school composition and theory
  strand (63 ids, loaded 2026-11-21; Cory, Desk q-fix-arts-1 and q-fix-downloads-1), as section 3.3 says.

## 2. Reuse terms (read at source, 2026-11-16)

Read on the coalition's own copyright guidelines (nationalartsstandards.org, "NCAS Copyright Guidelines", the
2025 posting of a document dated 2016). Research notes, not legal advice.

- The notice: the standards are copyright 2014 by the coalition, rights administered by Young Audiences, Inc., all
  rights reserved.
- Free and unlimited use covers only the eleven anchor standards and the blank framework matrix.
- Everything else (the performance standards, enduring understandings, essential questions, model cornerstone
  assessments, glossary) needs permission to reproduce, with set exceptions: teachers may copy the standards as
  shown on the site for their own teaching, and anyone may quote up to 250 words with the citation.
- Paraphrase is allowed in general, with the citation.
- What Curio Class does: the file and every plan carry ids and our own words only. No performance standard,
  enduring understanding or essential question is copied into a page, a day or a tool. Our own essential
  questions are written fresh. A page that names the standards gives the citation line in the notice's form.
- The model cornerstone assessments are not adapted. Our end-of-unit assessments are our own.
- A plan names a performance standard by id and states its action in one line of our own words. It never quotes
  the performance standards. The eleven anchors may be quoted in their real wording. Decided (Cory, 2026-11-20, Desk q-prework-arts-1):
  a one-line words file for the taught ids (id, tab, at most 14 words of what the student does, never a
  re-sentence of the standards' phrasing), written by one agent and read by a second, in the public repository; the
  63 strand ids joined it 2026-11-21, so it holds all 1,176 taught ids. The file now exists (data/standards/ncas-performance-standards-words.txt,
  written 2026-11-20): a plan cites the id and uses the file's line for the action.

## 3. The band-year rule

Our arts courses are bands: one course per discipline per band (visual arts, grades K to 2, and so on). The
standards are written grade by grade to grade 8, then in three high school levels. The rule below places them.

### 3.1 Kindergarten to grade 8: the grade's own standards in the grade's own year

| Band | Year 1 | Year 2 | Year 3 |
|---|---|---|---|
| K to 2 | Kindergarten ids (`.Ka` and so on) | Grade 1 ids | Grade 2 ids |
| 3 to 5 | Grade 3 ids | Grade 4 ids | Grade 5 ids |
| 6 to 8 | Grade 6 ids | Grade 7 ids | Grade 8 ids |

- Each year's scope and sequence lists every id of its grade in the discipline, unit by unit. Each id is taught
  and assessed in that year. The unit of count is the lettered id, one statement (`DA:Cr1.1.2a`); the table in
  3.2 gives each year's count. A year may revisit the year before's ids as practice that returns, but it claims
  only its own grade.
- Music's connecting ids (`MU:Cn10.1`, `MU:Cn11.1`) are one statement repeated in every grade, so their words lines
  match from K to 8; each grade meets them with its own music and words, and the progression row says what changes.
- Pre-kindergarten ids are not taught. A kindergarten unit may use one as a readiness reference.
- Mixed-grade classes are common in the arts. A school that teaches a band as one mixed class runs the three
  years in turn. Each year still claims only its grade's ids, so a student's record shows which grade's standards
  they met. The scope and sequence says this once, on its first page.

### 3.2 Grades 9 to 12: levels, not grades (Cory 2026-11-16, Desk card q-later-arts-health-pe)

The high school standards come in three levels, defined by what a student has done, not by grade (NAfME's
appendix to the music standards, read 2026-11-16, our words): Proficient is what most students reach in one high
school arts course of about 120 hours beyond good pre-kindergarten to grade 8 learning; Accomplished takes a
further sequence of courses; Advanced goes well past Accomplished, at honors or first-year college depth. The
evidence for the structure below is docs/reviews/arts-hs-music-and-levels-2026-11-16.md: no peer-reviewed study
compares sequenced and survey high school arts courses, so the structure follows the national descriptors and the
state frameworks that turn them into yearlong courses.

| Year of the 9 to 12 band | Level taught | Ids |
|---|---|---|
| Level 1 | Proficient | `.Ia` |
| Level 2 | Accomplished | `.IIa` |
| Level 3 | Advanced, at honors depth | `.IIIa` |
| Level 4 (optional) | Advanced, deepened: a portfolio or production year the student leads | `.IIIa`, assessed again on new, self-directed work |

- The 9 to 12 band is one course per discipline, as every other band is; its years are levels. A level is a
  full 160-day year, which is about the 120 hours the descriptor names.
- Entry: a student new to a discipline starts at Level 1, whatever their grade. The scope and sequence names the
  level, never a grade. Level 1's first unit assumes no earlier arts learning.
- Each level requires the one before, or a placement showing: the student shows work scored against the previous
  level's end-of-course assessment, and starts at the next level if it meets it.
- Advanced is claimed by assessed work, not by finishing Level 3. A student who ends Level 3 at "partly" has
  Level 4 to reach it.
- One level is a complete course and is enough for Proficient. The bar does not ask a school to offer all four.
- In data/progressions/arts.csv only, the `grade` column writes Level 1 as `9`, Level 2 as `10`, Level 3 as `11` and
  Level 4 as `12` (`~` only, on `.IIIa` ids), the key scripts/progression.py reads. It is a data key, never a
  student's grade: every frame, scope and sequence and page names the level. A row lists only the ids it owns or
  contributes to; earlier ids that return as practice are not listed.
- Short path (Cory, 2026-11-20, Desk q-prework-arts-1): each level's scope and sequence marks an 80-day path (a semester or a rotation) that keeps every
  unit and every id by merging the marked days. Level 1 opens with a bridge unit (Cory, 2026-11-21, Desk
  q-fix-arts-1): it teaches the grade 8 techniques and words Level 1 needs, as readiness, and claims no grade 8 id;
  it is at most 12 days in the full year and 6 days on the 80-day short path, and its days count toward the bar's 10
  percent limit on days outside the course's standards (6 of 80 is 7.5 percent). Levels 2 and 3 open with a review
  unit of at most 6 days that doubles as placement; review days count as inside the standards. The frame lists them
  as bridge or review days. The bridge's at most 12 days in the full 160-day year is settled (Cory, 2026-11-21, Desk
  q-fix-downloads-1): the same 7.5 percent share as its 6 days on the short path. The 6 days for review are ours,
  untested. The short
  path adds build work to the 15 high
  school levels (three in each of the five 9 to 12 courses), so many schools that cannot give an arts course every
  day can still use the course.

### Ids owned by each course-year (counted from the id file, 2026-11-19, music 9 to 12 on 2026-11-21; pre-kindergarten excluded)

| Discipline | K to 2 (K, 1, 2) | 3 to 5 | 6 to 8 | 9 to 12, per level (`.Ia`, `.IIa`, `.IIIa`; Level 4 repeats `.IIIa`) |
|---|---|---|---|---|
| Visual arts (VA) | 15 + 15 + 15 = 45 | 45 | 45 | 15 |
| Music (MU) | 18 + 20 + 20 = 58 | 63 | 63 | 21 each, strand `.C`, 63 in all (3.3) |
| Theater (TH) | 17 + 24 + 24 = 65 | 72 | 72 | 24 |
| Dance (DA) | 21 + 21 + 21 = 63 | 63 | 63 | 21 |
| Media arts (MA) | 18 + 18 + 18 = 54 | 54 | 54 | 18 |

An id is one lettered statement (`DA:Cr1.1.2a`). A year's scope and sequence claims every id of its grade in its
discipline, counted in this table. The file holds 1,264 ids: 1,176 taught (K to 8 and the high school levels) and 88
pre-kindergarten, not taught. Totals of taught ids: visual arts 180, music 247, theater 281, dance 252, media arts
216. The header's "per grade" figures are unlettered standards and are not the count. Music and theater
kindergarten are uneven (music K 18, grades 1 and 2 20, grades 3 to 8 21; theater K 17, grade 1 on 24), so no
year is counted by an even spread. High school music is one of the 20 courses, not an extra one; its 63 ids are
in the file since 2026-11-21.

### 3.3 Music in high school: composition and theory (Cory 2026-11-16, Desk card q-later-arts-health-pe)

Music 9 to 12 is a full composition and theory course on the national composition and theory strand, not a stub
(settled 2026-11-16, Cory, Desk card q-later-followups-1).
The reasons and sources are in docs/reviews/arts-hs-music-and-levels-2026-11-16.md.

- The strand starts at Proficient (no Novice or Intermediate levels), so Level 1 follows grade 8 general music
  directly. It runs to Advanced in the same three levels as 3.2.
- It needs no instrument: work is shared by notation, performance or technology, and performances may be live
  or recorded. Days use the voice (singing, sight-singing), body and found percussion, a free keyboard on any
  device or a Curio Class tool, and the student's own instrument when they have one.
- Ensemble, harmonizing instrument and music technology ids are not claimed. Those strands assume instruments,
  a group or production tools and a teacher who hears technique in the room.
- An ensemble companion page sits inside the course (no days, no ids, not a counted course): how to find a
  school or community ensemble, a practice log and self-check, how an assignment can be written for the
  student's own instrument or section, and a plain note that ensemble standards are met and assessed by that
  ensemble.
- Ids: 21 per level, 63 in all, format `MU:Cr1.1.C.Ia` (the strand letter `C` before the level); the connecting
  ids keep the handout's anchor `.0` (`MU:Cn10.0.C.Ia`, `MU:Cn11.0.C.Ia`). scripts/standards.py accepts the strand
  letter (read 2026-11-19). Loaded 2026-11-21 (Cory, Desk q-fix-arts-1 and q-fix-downloads-1), ids only, from the
  coalition's strand handout downloaded and read once; each has its line in the words file (same form and limits),
  and data/progressions/arts.csv has the music Level 1 to 4 rows (`mu-g9-invent` to `mu-g12-connect`) in the form
  of the other disciplines' rows (3.2). The counts in 3.2 include them.

### 3.4 Pacing (settled 2026-11-16)

The bar fails a plan that needs a daily arts period (criterion 9). A K to 8 arts year is 64 course days (two
sessions a week over 32 weeks, docs/courses/CALENDAR.md). Session length is 40 minutes (ours, untested); every
section plan states the set-up and clean-up minutes inside it. A school that meets once a week keeps every unit and
every standard on 32 days by merging the days each unit marks. High school levels are 160 course days of 45 minutes,
the 120 hours of the national level descriptor (3.2), and 60 hours on the 80-day short path (Cory, 2026-11-21, Desk
q-fix-arts-1). The 160 in docs/GLOSSARY.md is the school year, not a course's days, so nothing there changes.

National time figures (Cory, 2026-11-21, Desk q-fix-arts-1): each K to 8 course page names its national arts
association's recommended weekly time, read at source on the association's own site (a downloaded file is allowed,
Cory, 2026-11-21, Desk q-fix-downloads-1), and our gap (80 minutes a week, 40 on the short path), as health and
physical education do, so a school can add time. A figure not read at source stays off the page. Read 2026-11-21,
each from the association's opportunity-to-learn standards:

| Discipline | Recommended weekly time, K to 8 | Our gap (80 minutes a week; 40 on the short path) | Source |
|---|---|---|---|
| Dance | K to 2: at least 60 minutes (90 at the quality level); 3 to 5 and 6 to 8: at least 90 minutes | K to 2: 20 minutes over the 60 (20 short of it on the short path) and 10 short of the 90; 3 to 8: 10 minutes short (50 on the short path) | dance educators' association, opportunity-to-learn standards, 2017 revision, posted on nationalartsstandards.org |
| Visual arts, K to 5 | At least 50 minutes weekly through the year, taught by a certified visual arts educator | 30 minutes over (10 short on the short path) | visual arts educators' association, checklist for elementary visual arts education, 2025 posting on arteducators.org, pages 3 and 4 (read 2026-11-25) |
| Visual arts 6 to 8, and media arts | Pending(docs/reviews/frame-visual-arts-grades-k-to-2-fixes-2026-11-25.md#Left for the lead): the association's middle school checklist and its media arts lines are read at source before their frames; the 2026-11-21 read missed the elementary figure | none stated until read | visual arts educators' association, opportunity-to-learn checklists, 2025 posting on arteducators.org |
| Music | General music, basic level: at least 90 minutes in each of K to 2, 3 to 5 and 6 to 8 | 10 minutes short (50 on the short path) | music educators' association, National Opportunity to Learn Standards, 2020, on nafme.org (read in a browser 2026-11-21, nothing saved) |
| Theater | No figure: the theater association has no page giving one | none to state | none found |

The course pages carry the dance, music and visual arts K to 5 rows' figures and gaps; theater names none, since its association gives no minutes.

On the 32-session path each unit keeps four things, in this order and no fewer: the demonstration with a step card,
opened by the look or listen at a work that carries the unit's response to another artist's work and its
connecting sentence; one making or rehearsal day with a critique; the showing (the check); and one respond day.
Fluency days fold into the openers. The frame marks which days merge.

**The ordinary room (bar criterion 14).** A class of thirty needs a clear floor or tables, paper, pencils, found
material and the voice; nothing is bought, and every unit lists a cheap stand-in. A tool day has a printed version
that does the same job. The teacher needs no training in the discipline: each unit's teacher page says what to look
and listen for, in plain words. One child at home is the second room: the parent is the audience and the critique
partner, a showing is to the family or a recorded take, and a duet or group piece has a solo or doubled version.

## 4. Watching the national revision

The coalition announced a review in February 2025. Its March 2026 update (read 2026-11-16) reports a field survey
with 3,490 replies, a wish for refinement rather than a rewrite, and five aims: arts learning for all, more
concise wording, student voice and choice, technology and artificial intelligence, and readiness for life after
school. No draft date is given.

- Look twice a year, in the first week of May and the first week of November: the coalition's site (the review
  resources page and news) and its contact address for updates.
- Record each look here in one line: date, what changed, and whether any loaded id or this rule is affected.
- If revised standards are published: do not reload until the reuse terms are read again and Cory says so. Units
  already planned keep their 2014 ids, named as 2014.

| Date | What was seen | Effect |
|---|---|---|
| 2026-11-16 | March 2026 update: refinement, five aims, no draft or date | None |

## 5. Left out, and why

- What a school may leave out (Cory, 2026-11-20, Desk q-prework-arts-1): a school or family may leave out a unit
  or a work for its community's reasons (for example, a unit that uses dance, masks or play-acting); each such unit
  stands alone, and each course page says so plainly and names those units. That includes a unit that studies a
  sacred or ceremonial tradition: one line on each course page (Cory, 2026-11-21, Desk q-prework-open-1). Swapping
  a work for another keeps the unit's ids met; leaving out a unit means the course page and the unit's family note
  name the ids not claimed that year (Cory, 2026-11-21, Desk q-fix-arts-1).
- Pre-kindergarten ids: no pre-kindergarten courses.
- The high school ensemble, harmonizing instrument and music technology strands (3.3).
- Enduring understandings, essential questions, model cornerstone assessments and the glossary: permission
  needed to reproduce (section 2); we write our own.
- State arts standards: one national set is the reference. A state's own grade placement may differ; the course
  page says the national set is ours to follow.

## 6. Leans on the core progressions

These are places where an arts unit leans on, or gives to, a core course. The arts unit does not reteach the core
skill. Each lean is named, with the core row or code it needs, in the `cross_subject` column of
data/progressions/arts.csv, which scripts/progression.py checks against the core files; the codes below are those
rows.

- Music and math: beat, note values and measures lean on fractions (math 3.NF.A.1, 4.NF.A.1, 5.NF.A.1). A music
  unit on note values is placed after the math week that teaches fractions (data/plans/calendar.csv), or uses beat
  counts only.
- Visual arts and math: shape, symmetry, scale and proportion lean on geometry and ratio (math 4.G.A.3, 7.G.A.1).
- Theater and ELA: script reading and character lean on the reading literature standards (RL.K.3 to RL.7.3);
  performing leans on the speaking and listening standards (SL.K.6 to SL.8.4). Responding in every art uses ELA's
  class talk and claim-and-evidence writing (SL.3.1, SL.6.1, W.5.1 to W.11-12.1).
- Dance and physical education: locomotor and nonlocomotor movement lean on the PE motor skills (SHAPE.S1; the PE
  rows physical-education:k-in-place to g9-dance-design, one per grade of dance technique). Dance
  in arts is the art: making, performing, responding to and connecting dance. Dance in physical education is
  movement skill, fitness and teamwork. Arts dance units do not teach the motor skill; PE dance units do not teach
  choreography, performing for an audience or response to dance. Each pair of units names the other in its
  connections line.
- Media arts and computer science: media arts teaches the one idea of privacy or copyright its unit needs, in a
  short step of the unit, and notes the computer science unit that goes deeper
  (computer-science:g8-attribution); it does not wait for it.
- All arts and social studies: works are taught with their time and place (bar criterion 7), using social
  studies' sourcing habits (social-studies:ss6-paleolithic-neolithic, ss6-silk-roads-exchange).
# Arts: approach

**Approved by Cory 2026-11-20 (Desk q-prework-arts-1), with the cold read's fixes in
(docs/reviews/prework-arts-read-2026-11-19.md); field labels and plain errors fixed 2026-11-21
(docs/reviews/prework-arts-fixes-2026-11-20.md).** This is the layer 3 approach page for the five arts (visual arts,
music, theater, dance, media arts). It fills the unit and section plans' subject slots (docs/courses/UNIT-PLAN.md)
under the shared page docs/APPROACH.md. The standard, the id counts, pacing, the high school levels and the music
strand are in docs/courses/arts/STANDARD.md; the bar is the arts part of docs/ADOPTION-BAR-SUBJECTS.md; the lens is
docs/agents/lenses/arts.md. Rules live in STANDARD.md and are not repeated here.

Each heading names the UNIT-PLAN.md field it fills. Fields not named (unit fields 1 and 3, section fields 2 and 11)
follow UNIT-PLAN.md with nothing added for the arts, except that a 9 to 12 header names the level, never a grade
(STANDARD.md 3.2), and the boundaries of STANDARD.md section 6 (dance and PE, music and math, media arts and computer
science) are named in section field 1's connections and section field 11.

## What the arts carry (unit field 2)

- **The standards and their parts.** The unit lists each performance standard by id with its parts, one part per
  action, in one line of our own words (STANDARD.md section 2), and the limit (for example, "with a given pattern,
  not an invented one"). Every part has an end-of-unit item. The three things below sit under this.

An arts unit also carries three things, named in the unit plan:

- **The technique**: one skill of the discipline taught in steps that get harder (a brush stroke, a rhythm
  pattern, a vocal warm-up, a stage cross, a dance phrase, a camera shot).
- **The works studied**: two to four works by other artists (see "Works and their settings" and "Rights").
- **The showing**: what the class makes and shows or performs at the end, and to whom (the class, another class,
  families).

**Works and their settings.** Each year's works include living makers and more than one region and period; a unit
plan lists each work with its maker, place, date, purpose and rights line. A setting is when, where, by whom and
for what. No single tradition is the default. Where the open copy of a work is thin, the unit uses a description
and a link to the maker's or museum's own page, never a copy, and does not drop the work. Where a work has a sacred
or ceremonial use, the unit says what it is, studies it by looking and listening only, and does not have students
perform or imitate it. Students learn a tradition's form from the community's own published teaching or a named
practitioner's words, never by costume or invented imitation, and no student plays a character by an accent or a
stereotype. The unit plan records the works' makers and places in one line each. Every unit gets the fresh-agent
sensitivity read, which covers the works with the rest of the unit (docs/pipeline/briefs/sensitivity-read.md).
Museum open-access images and public domain recordings lean to older, European and American works, so the year's
list is checked for range, not taken as the open sources give it (docs/reviews/arts-open-material-2026-11-16.md).

**Rights.** Every work a unit uses has a rights line in the unit plan, checked on the item's own page. A work that
is not public domain or openly licensed is described in our own words and linked to the maker's or museum's page,
never copied or embedded. Scripts, songs and dances students perform are public domain, openly licensed, original
to Curio Class or written by the students. A showing for families uses only those. Media arts remix uses only
public domain or openly licensed sources and names them. A student's work is the student's: it is shown outside the
class only with the family's yes, and carries the student's choice of name.

## Kinds of day (principle 2)

The five kinds of day are the same as in every subject. In the arts:

- **Understanding.** A technique is shown and named in steps, or a work is studied with the discipline's words
  (look, listen, describe, interpret). The artistic process "responding to a work" lives here: it is a teaching
  day, not a respond day. Connecting (Cn10, Cn11) is taught as each id's words line asks: an id that asks for a tie
  or a study (the student's own life, or the work's time, place or purpose) lives in the understanding day that
  studies a work, in the day's evidence sentence; an id that asks the student to make, research or perform (for
  example `VA:Cn10.1.Ka`, `DA:Cn10.1.6b`) lives in an application day and is scored as making. The unit plan's
  parts list says which.
- **Fluency.** Short, repeated practice of a technique with feedback: rhythms, scales, sketchbook drills, warm-ups,
  a phrase run several times, a scene beat repeated.
- **Application.** Students make, rehearse or produce their own work using the technique, with some choice. The
  second application day holds the unit's one critique (see "Critique"); there is no extra day.
- **Check.** A showing or performance scored with a guide (see "Scoring creative work"), or a response to a work
  not studied in the unit. The end-of-unit assessment is always a check day.
- **Respond.** Acts on a check: a second try at the part marked "not yet", or a re-rehearsal before a second
  showing.

A unit runs roughly: understanding, fluency, application, fluency, application (with the critique), check,
respond. Every unit has at least one showing (bar criterion 4) and one response to another artist's work
(criterion 5). The 32-session path, which keeps four things of each unit, is in STANDARD.md 3.4.

## Readiness and the baseline look (unit field 4)

A student new to the band, or to the year, takes the year's first unit as written; each unit's opening retrieval or
readiness task names the earlier-year technique it assumes and the ten-minute reteach (UNIT-PLAN field 4, whose
timing rules hold). The baseline look under "Growth" is an observation during the unit's first practice days, not a
readiness check, so day 1 stays clean.

## Scoring creative work (unit field 5)

- **The guide** has three to five look-fors, drawn from the unit's technique and words, each scored met, partly or
  not yet. It never marks talent, speed, neatness, effort, the maker's identity, or how the work compares with
  another student's. A student's choice of subject is never marked down. Students score their own work with the
  same guide first.
- **Samples.** A sample is a written description of a work or performance at each level, or a work the team made as
  an adult example. Samples at two levels sit in the unit plan beside the look-fors.
- **The check day.** Performances are shown in small groups on the check day, and any group the teacher cannot
  watch live is recorded that day (under "Kept work stays on the device or on paper"). The teacher scores from the
  live showing and the recordings before the respond day, which starts from the marks.
- **Unseen work.** In visual arts, music and media arts, the unseen work is a public domain or openly licensed work
  or a Curio Class original (see "Rights"). In dance and theater, it is a short written description, a live
  demonstration by the teacher or a Curio Class original, never an embed.
- **Growth.** Each unit opens with a baseline look at the unit's main look-for (rotating groups, recorded met,
  partly or not yet) and ends with the same look at the check, so each student has two marks and the growth is the
  change. Kept work becomes a portfolio: one piece a unit, with its one-line reflection, kept in a folder (paper, a
  file or a photo).

## Errors (unit field 6 and section field 4)

As UNIT-PLAN.md. An arts letter is one technique or reading error (for example, a steady beat taken for the
rhythm), marked "from memory" unless a source is named.

## Marking and response (unit field 7)

Items are teacher-judged; groups for the respond day come from the record sheet, at most four, and the others work
in pairs from the written move (the social studies form of unit field 7).

## Tools, supplies and safe use (unit field 8; instruments and a room with nothing in section field 5)

**What a tool may and may not do.** "Tool" means Curio Class software only; paint, instruments and the like are
materials. A tool may show a technique step by step, give practice with feedback on things that can be checked
(rhythm reading, note names, color mixing, shot types, stage directions), and keep work on the student's own
device. It may not judge a finished work or a performance, stand in for the performing in the room, or generate the
student's work. A tool for color names every color in words and in numbers and works with no color judgment alone;
a tool for sound shows the beat on screen and works with the sound off; every tool plays by keyboard.

**Supplies.** Materials and instruments that cost money are never required; a free or found-material option is
always given. The room, class size and cost answer is in STANDARD.md 3.4.

**Safe use.** The unit lists the clear space, the surface, the equipment and the hazard (criterion 9), and every
unit with hands-on work gets the fresh-agent safety read (docs/pipeline/briefs/sensitivity-read.md). Rules for every
course, all from memory until that read checks them. Dance: a floor with room to move arms out, soft-soled or bare
feet on a clean floor, no lifts, no jumping from heights, partner contact only by agreement and never required.
Theater: no real weapons, no contact stage fights, props checked, falls not rehearsed. Visual arts: blades only by
an adult, no heat sources for children below grade 6, powdered clay and dyes with ventilation, wash hands,
materials labeled non-toxic, no sharing of face-contact items. Music: volume limits for headphones and drums, no
loud sound in a student's ear, hearing and voice rest, no forced singing alone. Media arts: no filming a student
without the family's yes, no flashing lights, screen breaks. A safety statement without a source is marked "from
memory".

## Family note (unit field 9)

Each finished unit carries a family note, in English and Spanish. It is where the family's yes is asked for a
showing outside class (see "Rights"), and, for a unit a school leaves out, where the ids not claimed that year are
named (STANDARD.md 5).

## Sources (unit field 10)

Field 10 names where each work's rights line was checked (see "Rights"). Nothing here has been tried with children,
and Curio Class makes no claim that arts study raises any other result. Each course names a one-page logic model in
its frame.

## Practices (section field 1)

Each section names the artistic process or processes it works on (create, perform/present/produce, respond,
connect) and the anchor standards by code. Over a year every course touches all four processes (criterion 1). The
teacher page says what to look and listen for, in plain words (STANDARD.md 3.4).

## The daily evidence (principle 8; section field 3)

The form of evidence depends on the day:

- **A making day** ends with the work in its current state, kept (a photo of the page, a short recording, a saved
  file), plus one line by the student: what technique I used and what I will change. A K to 2 student says it to
  the teacher or a partner instead of writing it.
- **A study day** ends with one written or spoken sentence about the work that must use one of the day's words
  and point to something in the work.
- **A performing day** ends with a short self-check against one look-for, and the teacher's mark of met, partly or
  not yet for a few named students (a rotating list, so every student is seen within the unit).

The teacher sorts the evidence into met, partly and not yet, with no mark, and the plan says what the next
session's first minutes do for "not yet". Over the year the kept work becomes a portfolio (criterion 8).

**Kept work stays on the device or on paper.** A recording, photo or saved file stays on the student's, family's or
school's device and goes nowhere else. A recording may be of hands, the work or the sound alone; a face or voice is
never required. A student who does not want to be recorded is observed live and the teacher's mark stands as the
evidence.

## What is carried (section field 5)

The technique is shown by the teacher in named steps (the teacher holds it), tried together with a step card (the
class holds it with the card), then made alone with the card in hand, then made with the card withdrawn. Each
section plan names the day the step card goes and the day a student uses the steps from memory. A model work or
demonstration is copied as a step, never as the end, and the card goes before the check. A technique is never first
met in independent work. In the section plan the verb is: shown, carried with a step card, withdrawn. A room with
nothing uses the printed step card (STANDARD.md 3.4).

## How a day opens (section field 6)

Every day opens in the first five minutes with one of three things, named in the day row: a warm-up from earlier
technique (a rhythm, a warm-up, a sketch drill), a look or listen at a work with no prior knowledge asked (taught
on the same day), or the set-up routine. A making day opens with the demonstration of the step, never with a free
choice. Teaching first is the default for anything new in every grade (UNIT-PLAN rule 13); no opening task asks for
a technique not yet taught. The day row's minutes include set-up and clean-up (STANDARD.md 3.4). A timer may be used
for a set-up and clean-up cue (classroom management, rule 14).

## Critique (section field 7)

A critique follows one routine, always in this order: describe what you see or hear, ask a question about a choice,
suggest one change tied to a look-for of the unit. Feedback names a look-for; praise alone is not feedback. Students
revise and say in one line what they changed. Each unit has one critique before the check, inside the second
application day, and the check scores work revised after it. The respond day acts on the check as before.

## Practice that returns (section field 8)

Techniques return in the next unit's fluency openers and in later years at a harder step. The discipline's words
come back in every response. Each year revisits the year before's grade ids as practice, never as a claim.

## Supports (section field 9)

- Every making task has more than one way in: a choice of material, size, instrument part or role.
- Every performing task has a way to take part without performing alone (ensemble, small group, recorded take).
- **English learners.** Each unit teaches three to five discipline words in depth, with a picture, a movement or a
  sound and a frame ("I see ___ because ___." "The rhythm goes ___." "My character wants ___."). A student may show
  or perform the word instead of saying it. The unit's check marks what the student made, showed or performed
  separately from what they said, so a language gap is not marked as a skill gap.
- **Access.** Each section names what changes for a student who cannot see (describe the work aloud, touch models,
  large print step cards), cannot hear (a visual beat, a floor vibration, a written cue), cannot move freely (seated
  or one-limb versions, a role such as director or sound lead), or is sensitive to noise, touch, light or crowds
  (hearing protection, gloves, brush grips, a quiet corner, no forced contact, no strobe).
- **Reading load.** Step cards are picture-led with the words read aloud on the card for grades K to 2. A student
  reads at most one short card alone in grades 3 to 5.
- **Extension, same standard.** A harder pattern, a second voice or layer, a tighter constraint, or teaching the
  step to a partner. Never the next grade's standard.

## Two rooms (section field 10)

One child at home is in STANDARD.md 3.4 (the parent is the audience and the critique partner). One projector or none:
every day runs from printed step cards, and a screen only shows a work, a step or a playback.

## Sensitive ground

These rules hold in every arts course (Cory, 2026-11-21, Desk q-fix-arts-1). A section that touches one carries its
teacher note in section field 1.

- **Diet, weight and body.** No task asks about a student's own diet, weight or body. The dance health-habit ids
  (`DA:Pr5.1.4b` to `DA:Pr5.1.IIIb`) study food choices, eating plans and body care through an invented dancer or
  dancers in general; a student's own habits come in only as warm-up, rest and safe practice, by the student's choice.
- **Ties to the student's own life.** Every task that ties art to the student's own life (Cn10) offers an invented
  or public option beside it, such as an invented person, a story character or a public figure, and the option is
  marked the same.
- **If a student tells you something.** Each section carries health's steps (docs/courses/health/APPROACH.md, "If a
  student tells you something") on its teacher page.

## Pacing

See STANDARD.md 3.4: the days, the 40-minute session (45 minutes in 9 to 12), the 32-session path, the national
time figures and the ordinary room.

## High school levels and music

The levels, entry, placement and the music strand's rules are in STANDARD.md 3.2 and 3.3. What the plans fill:
Choice grows by level (Level 1 sets the problems, Level 2 lets the student pick among them and research, Level 3
has the student set the problem). Level 4 is one sustained project with checkpoints as check days and critique as
respond days. High school music is composing and theory, practiced by voice, body percussion and a free keyboard or
Curio Class tool; a day may offer "use your own instrument" as one way in, and listening and score study carry the
responding standards.

## Evidence and sources (C13, C16)

| Source | How it was read |
|---|---|
| Texas fine arts rubric, 2025 | in full by the bar's writer |
| California's five arts categories | in full by the bar's writer |
| Oklahoma's fine arts rubric, 2024 | in full by the expert |
| National Core Arts Standards, 2014 | ids and the free anchors (STANDARD.md); music handout rechecked 2026-11-21 |
| Music composition and theory strand handout, 2021-11 | downloaded and read once 2026-11-21, ids only (STANDARD.md 3.3) |
| Opportunity-to-learn standards of the dance and the visual and media arts associations | downloaded and read once 2026-11-21, weekly figures only (STANDARD.md 3.4); the music association's file was not read, the theater association gives no figure |
| NCAS "Standards Process Updates for the Field", March 2026 | read 2026-11-16 (STANDARD.md section 4) |
| NAfME descriptors for high school levels | in full, 2026-11-16 (docs/reviews/arts-hs-music-and-levels-2026-11-16.md) |
| Open material for the works studied | docs/reviews/arts-open-material-2026-11-16.md |
| From memory | the safe use rules (marked so under "Safe use") and the describe, ask, suggest critique routine (the lens's background, not a cited source) |
# Course frame: Theater, grades 6 to 8

Course `theater-grades-6-to-8` (catalogue id `theater-6-8`), a band course for grade 6, grade 7 and grade 8, run as three years one after another (year 6, year 7, year 8). Draft frame, written 2026-11-25; the scope and sequence sets the days later, one year at a time, and each year's part of this file is that year's frame (data/courses.csv, "one frame per grade year").

## The course in brief

- **Units.** 12: four in each year. Every year has the same four-unit shape, so a technique and a thread come back
  one step harder a year later in the same place: a character unit (the actor's tools and the written response to
  artists' choices), a staging and story unit (fixing a staging problem in a scene and studying the play's versions,
  era and objects), a design and judging unit (the scene's tech and a judgment against criteria), and a devised
  showing unit (a group piece on a shared theme or community issue, with the cultural views that shape how drama is
  judged) that ends the year with a showing for an audience. Each unit joins two progression threads, one of
  creating or performing and one of responding or connecting, so every unit makes, performs, studies another
  artist's work and shows (bar criteria 4 and 5). Grades 6 to 8 theater has eight rows a year, two a unit.
- **Days.** 192: 64 a year for three years. Each year: 60 days in its four units, 2 course-level check days (one
  mid-year, one year-end) and 2 respond days, one after each check. One session of 40 minutes, two sessions a week,
  32 weeks a year (STANDARD.md 3.4: a K to 8 arts year is 64 course days; it never needs a daily period, bar
  criterion 9). The 40 minutes hold the set-up routine (about 3 minutes: chairs and bags to the walls, the clear floor
  checked, the group agreements card up), the warm-up (5), the teaching, rehearsing and making, and the close (about
  4: chairs back, props and scripts to the class box, the day's evidence collected); a making session with card,
  tape and paint takes 6 for clean-up. A readiness or retrieval task takes ten minutes. Each unit's days include its
  end-of-unit assessment (two check days: a showing day and a written day; no unit is over 25 days, so none has a
  mid-unit assessment) and one respond day after it. Unit counts follow the rows' load: the character unit is
  longest (18 days) because it owns seven ids, sets up the year's warm-up and floor routine and retrieves two rows;
  the other three units own five or six ids each and take 14 days, the devised unit's devising and showing balanced
  by the year's characters, fixes and designs already made. These are course days: the school calendar
  (docs/courses/CALENDAR.md) spreads them two a week, so course day n of a year falls in that year's week n / 2,
  rounded up. National time figure (STANDARD.md 3.4): none is stated, because the 2026-11-21 read found no theater
  association page giving minutes; the second-model prework read cites a middle school figure from the theater
  educators' association that is not read at source (For the lead item 2). The course gives 80 minutes a week (about
  43 hours a year) and 40 on the once-a-week path. The course is written so a teacher with no drama training can
  teach it; a school with a drama teacher uses that teacher.
- **The once-a-week path.** A school that meets once a week keeps every unit and every id in 32 sessions a year by
  merging the days each unit marks (STANDARD.md 3.4). Each unit keeps, in this order and no fewer: the demonstration
  with a step card, opened by the watch or read of a work that carries the unit's response and its connecting
  sentence; one rehearsal day with the critique; the showing (the check); one respond session, whose first 20
  minutes hold the unit's written items before the second try. Fluency days fold into the openers, and the two
  rows' understanding days merge into one. Sessions on that path, every year: units of 9, 7, 6 and 6; each year's
  two checks keep 1 check session and 1 respond session. The mid-year check session falls in week 17 and its respond
  session in week 18; the year-end check session in week 31 and its respond session in week 32. A merged day never
  drops a critique, a revision or a respond day; the scope and sequence marks which days merge.
- **Standard.** National Core Arts Standards, 2014, theater performance standards, by id only, each named with its
  line in data/standards/ncas-performance-standards-words.txt, never quoted (STANDARD.md 2). The band-year rule binds
  (STANDARD.md 3.1): year 6 claims only the 24 grade 6 ids (`.6a`, `.6b`, `.6c`), year 7 only the 24 grade 7 ids,
  year 8 only the 24 grade 8 ids: 72 in all, the count in STANDARD.md 3.2, every one taught and assessed in its own
  year. A year revisits the year before's ids as practice that returns, never as a claim. Every year touches all
  four artistic processes (creating, performing, responding, connecting; bar criterion 1), and each unit touches at
  least two. In the Units table the C3 column is headed "Processes, anchors and works": the artistic processes and
  anchor standards the unit works on, and the works it studies. Each id's action was checked against its words-file
  line and the row's description; the national text governs where they part (a unit plan that finds a drift names
  it under its own For the lead). Check set: each unit plan checks itself against the words-file lines of its ids,
  the nine criteria of the arts part of docs/ADOPTION-BAR-SUBJECTS.md and the lens's error list
  (docs/agents/lenses/arts.md); the "Check set adds" column names what that is expected to add. Values rule
  (DESK-ANSWERS 2026-11-12) holds. scripts/standards.py checks every id against the id file (L83, done 2026-11-21);
  each year's scope and sequence still states its id count (24) against STANDARD.md 3.2.
- **Mixed-grade classes.** A school that teaches the band as one mixed class runs the three years in turn (STANDARD.md
  3.1); each year still claims only its grade's ids, so a student's record shows which grade's standards they met.
  The scope and sequence says this once, on its first page.
- **Base text.** None. Theater is Curio Class's own (docs/sources/BASE-TEXTS.md; the open-material scout,
  docs/reviews/arts-open-material-2026-11-16.md, found few open theater recordings). Works studied are of four
  kinds: plays whose texts are public domain (Goldoni, Chekhov, Kalidasa in its 1855 and 1912 English translations,
  Wilde, the Yuan Chalk Circle in its 1832 French translation), from which Curio Class adapts one short scene in its
  own words with the source credited; a public domain film (Chaplin, 1916); theater forms, companies and makers,
  living and past, described in our own words and linked to the maker's, a museum's, a university's or a cultural
  body's own page, never embedded or copied; and open-access museum objects and prints, with their rights line.
  Scripts the class performs are these adaptations, Curio Class originals or the students' own (APPROACH "Rights").
  Unseen check work is a short written description, a live teacher performance of a Curio Class original scene, or a
  classmates' fresh scene (APPROACH "Scoring creative work"). Each work named below is a pick the unit plan keeps
  unless its rights line, checked on the item's own page, fails; a swapped work keeps the unit's ids met and the
  year's range. Range, checked across the band and within each year: year 6 Africa (Ghana), the Americas (the United
  States), Asia (Japan), Europe (Italy, England, Switzerland) and Oceania (Aotearoa New Zealand); year 7 Africa
  (Kenya), the Americas (a Native American company of Kuna and Rappahannock founders; Chicago; New York), Asia (India,
  Japan) and Europe (Russia, the United Kingdom); year 8 Africa (South Africa), the Americas (Brazil; a Native
  American playwrights' company; the United States), Asia (China), Europe (England, Russia) and Oceania (an
  Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander company); living makers in every year. No pick repeats a K to 2 theater work
  (kamishibai, Chinese shadow puppetry, an Akan tale, mamulengo, bunraku, the Panchatantra, Peking opera, a Cherokee
  story). Curio Class writes every step card, scene adaptation, research card, object card, description, record sheet
  and family note.
- **Techniques that get harder (bar criterion 2).** Acting: body choices from a character's given circumstances (6),
  a character's goal played two ways with an inner monologue (7), tactics switched at a beat against an obstacle,
  with a bodily response (8). Staging: two or three fixes for one staging problem tested in rehearsal (6), fixes from
  several viewpoints and a written defense (7), pacing tried and an original idea built on researched background (8).
  Design: one design problem fixed and the plan tried in rehearsal (6), several elements chosen and weighed together
  (7), elements combined for the real performance space and refined with simple technology (8). Devising: offers
  built on and a piece adapted for an informal showing (6), many community voices woven with another art and every
  rehearsal kept (7), lead roles rotated, a piece reworked by runs and staged polished (8). Responding: a written
  reaction (6), reactions compared (7), decisions judged against criteria (8).
- **Day kinds.** As docs/GLOSSARY.md ("teaching day") and APPROACH "Kinds of day". Understanding: a technique shown in
  named steps with the step card (a body choice, a beat change, a cue called), or a work watched, read or described
  and studied with the unit's words (the artistic process "responding" is taught here, never on a respond day).
  Fluency: the warm-up routine and a scene beat run several times with feedback (a voice and body warm-up, a status
  walk, a tactic switch drill, a cue run); mostly the five-minute warm-up and one or two fluency days a unit.
  Application: rehearsal, devising and design making with some choice of role, line, fix or element; the second
  rehearsal day holds the unit's one critique (describe, ask, suggest), and the next day's revision is application
  too. Check: the showing day and the written day of the unit's evidence line. Respond: a second try at the part
  marked "not yet", or a re-rehearsal before a second showing. A unit runs roughly: understanding, fluency,
  application, fluency, application with the critique, revision, two check days, respond.
- **Scored evidence.** Every owned id has an end-of-unit item or a look-for on the unit's guide, named id by id in the
  unit's "End-of-unit evidence" line. A progression row's "Assessed by" note names one form for the row, not one item
  for each id, so it is never the whole line. Items come in three kinds: a look-for on the showing's guide (three to
  five look-fors, met, partly or not yet), marked live as each group performs; a kept sheet (a character map, an
  idea sheet, a script markup, a cue sheet, a design sketch with its reason, a rehearsal log, a critique-and-revise
  note) made on the unit's last rehearsal or making days, scored after the session; and written items on the written
  day, answered on an unseen work. The end-of-unit assessment is two check days near the unit's end, so the teacher
  never marks a performance and reads or hears other work at once. The showing day's 40 minutes: set-up 3; warm-up
  5; six groups of five perform for about 4 minutes each (24) while the others watch as the audience; each student's
  self-check on the guide 3; close 4. That is 39 minutes. The written day's 40 minutes: set-up 2; the teacher
  performs the unit's short unseen Curio Class scene twice or hands out the unseen description (6); pairs talk for 3
  minutes where an id asks for discussion; the written items (25); close 4. That is 40 minutes, with no paint, glue or
  floor work. A group the teacher cannot watch live is recorded on the school's device that day, hands, props and
  sound only where the family has said no to faces, and marked before the respond day (APPROACH "Kept work"). The
  showing's rehearsals, its spoken critique with the teacher's look-for marks on the record sheet, and its revision
  come on earlier days counted in the unit. No score comes from the day a technique is first taught. Each unit opens
  with a baseline look at its main look-for during the first practice days (APPROACH "Growth"), so every student has
  two marks a unit and the growth is the change. One piece a unit goes into the portfolio folder (a character map, a
  cue sheet, a design sketch, a log page or a photo of the staging) with a one-line written reflection (bar criterion
  8).
- **Readiness and retrieval.** Every year 6 unit assumes the grade 5 row in each of its two threads, last used one
  grade back in Theater, grades 3 to 5, so it opens with a retrieval task inside day 2's opener, never a readiness
  check, and day 1 stays clean for the hook and the question. Theater, grades 3 to 5 is not a core course, so the
  seam is by course order with its fallback (lesson 32): each year 6 retrieval task carries its ten-minute reteach of
  the grade 5 technique as written, for any student new to theater (APPROACH "Readiness and the baseline look").
  Every year 7 and year 8 unit assumes the year before's row in the same thread, last used one grade back in this
  course, so it opens the same way, with the unit and days of that last use named in its row. The count starts from
  the band's order (6, then 7, then 8). In a mixed class running the years in turn, a student may meet year 8 before
  year 7: each retrieval task then carries its ten-minute reteach as written. The Seams tables give each row's last
  use.
- **Course-level checks.** Grades 6 to 8 after-core checks keep off the school days that hold a core check, and this
  course keeps off whole core check weeks (grade 6: weeks 12, 15, 16, 18, 22 to 24; grade 7: 9, 12 to 14, 22, 24;
  grade 8: 10, 14, 15, 18, 20, 24 to 26, 29 and 30; week 32 is every grade's finals week, CALENDAR.md stagger rule 3).
  Chosen by the units' load, not to dodge other after-core courses (the lead's calendar pass does that): mid-year in
  week 17 in every year (course days 33 and 34 of the year, school days 83 and 85, after units 1 and 2's 32 days;
  grade 6's math respond day is school day 81, two days earlier); year-end in week 32, the finals week, in every year
  (course days 63 and 64, school days 158 and 160), after the devised showing unit, whose showing is its own
  end-of-unit assessment. Each check day: set-up 3, warm-up 3, the groups plan a fresh task from a new card (8), six
  groups of five perform it for about 3 minutes each (18) while each student in the audience writes the check's
  written item on one assigned group's scene, close 4. That is 36 minutes; the teacher marks one group at a time and
  hears no talk that day.
- **Leave-out.** A school or family may leave out a unit or a work for its community's reasons (STANDARD.md 5); each
  unit stands alone. Every unit of this course uses play-acting, which STANDARD.md 5 names as a reason a community may
  leave a unit out; a school that leaves out play-acting leaves out the course, and the course page says so plainly.
  Units that study a cultural or ceremonial tradition, named on the course page: unit 1 (kyogen), unit 4 (a M�?ori
  theater company; a Ghanaian play's town festival), unit 6 (Sanskrit drama and the hermitage rites, studied by
  reading only), unit 7 (kabuki), unit 8 (a Native American company's storyweaving; a Kenyan community theater),
  unit 10 (a Yuan troupe painted in a temple hall, studied by looking only), unit 11 (an Aboriginal and Torres Strait
  Islander company) and unit 12 (a South African play's Christian imagery; a Native American playwrights' company).
  Swapping a work keeps the unit's ids met; leaving a unit out means the course page and the unit's family note name
  the ids not claimed that year.
- **The ordinary room.** A class of 30 with a clear floor (desks pushed back, a hall or a cleared corner), chairs,
  printed scenes, card, tape, found objects, the room's own light switch and blinds, and the voice; nothing must be
  bought, and every unit names a free stand-in (STANDARD.md 3.4, APPROACH "Supplies"). One student at home: the parent
  is the audience and the critique partner, a group scene has a doubled version (parent and student, or the student
  playing two roles with a chair for the other), and the showing is to the family or a recorded take. The teacher
  needs no drama training: each unit's teacher page says what to look and listen for, in plain words.
- **Supplies and cost.** Provisional cost per unit for a class of 30 (consumables, reusable items counted once,
  printing of scenes, cards and sheets), all optional beside the free path: year 6 $5, $5, $30 (six small LED
  flashlights, reusable for the band, colored film, card), $5 ($45); year 7 $5, $5, $15 (film and card), $5 ($30);
  year 8 $5, $5, $15 (film, card, tape), $5 ($30). Planning figures, not quotes; any ceiling is under For the lead.
- **Devices.** None per student. One teacher screen may show a work's page or a step; every day runs from the printed
  step card, scene and description without it. A school device may record a group the teacher cannot watch live
  (Scored evidence) and may play a sound cue in units 7 and 11. Owned ids that ask for technology: TH:Cr3.1.8c
  ("simple technology"), met in full with flashlights or the room's light switch and a found-object sound table run
  from a cue sheet, so a paper-path student meets every id in full. Kept work stays on paper or on the school's or
  family's device (APPROACH "Kept work").
- **Paper path.** Every day runs on the floor, the voice, paper, objects and printed cards; any Curio Class tool a unit
  plan adds (a stage-directions practice tool, a cue-calling practice tool) has a printed version that does the same
  job and needs no student account.
- **Reading load.** Scenes are short (one to two pages for each group) and are read aloud first at a table read; a
  student may take a role with fewer lines, a role with no lines, or the stage manager's, designer's or director's
  job, and the check marks performing apart from reading. Research cards are a page at most, at the grade's reading
  level. Written items ask for one to eight sentences. English learners: each unit teaches its six words in depth,
  with a picture, a gesture or a sound and a frame ("My character wants ___ so she ___." "I judge ___ by ___
  because ___."), a student may show or perform the word instead of saying it, and the check marks what the student
  performed apart from what they wrote (APPROACH "Supports").
- **Safety read.** Every unit is hands-on (movement on a shared floor; prop, set and light making), so every unit gets
  the fresh safety agent's read (docs/pipeline/briefs/sensitivity-read.md) against free guides, named here and opened
  by the agent: the federal consumer product safety agency's "Art and Craft Safety Guide" (cpsc.gov) and California's
  environmental health hazard office's guidance on art and craft materials for schools, with its list of materials
  not to buy for grades K to 6 (oehha.ca.gov; this course applies it to grades 7 and 8 too), for every making day;
  and the same federal agency's button battery safety pages (cpsc.gov), for the flashlights. Rules for every unit,
  from APPROACH "Safe use" and marked "from memory" until that read checks them: a clear floor with room for arms
  out, chairs and bags to the walls; soft soles or bare feet on a clean floor; no lifts, no falls rehearsed, no
  contact stage fights and no real or toy weapons; partner contact only by agreement and never required; voice rest
  and no forced loud sound; props checked by the teacher before use (no sharp edges, nothing breakable, no cords long
  enough to wrap a neck); no ladders, no standing on chairs or tables, nothing hung above heads; flashlights LED only,
  with screw-closed battery doors and no coin or button batteries; no light shone into eyes and no flashing; no heat
  source or hot glue; blades used by an adult only; supplies labeled non-toxic; any device cable taped down; hands
  washed after paint, glue and found objects. Each unit's last column gives its hazards and controls in the words
  days.csv will copy. No student sign-off form.
- **Sensitivity reads.** Every unit gets the fresh sensitivity agent's read (docs/pipeline/briefs/sensitivity-read.md),
  which covers the works with the rest of the unit; no outside reader is planned (Cory, 2026-11-14). Units with a
  cultural or community tradition name the communities and the sources the agent studies first: unit 1 (kyogen of
  Japan), unit 2 (commedia dell'arte of Italy), unit 4 (Efua Sutherland's Ghana; Taki Rua and te ao M�?ori), unit 5
  (Lorraine Hansberry's Chicago and the housing covenant case), unit 6 (Sanskrit drama of India), unit 7 (kabuki of
  Japan), unit 8 (Spiderwoman Theater's Kuna and Rappahannock founders; Kamiriithu of Kenya), unit 10 (Yuan drama of
  China), unit 11 (Ilbijerri's Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander communities) and unit 12 (Woza Albert! and
  apartheid South Africa; Native Voices at the Autry). No student plays a character by an accent, a costume of
  another culture or a stereotype; no student is cast against their wish; no role play puts a student in a victim's
  or a harmer's place, and no scene stages bullying, abuse or harassment (APPROACH "Works and their settings").
  Every social problem or community issue comes from a class list of local, non-partisan public matters or an
  invented town, marked the same (values rule), and never from a student's own family, health, body or a disclosure.
  Every task that ties a character or a choice to a student's own life or beliefs (units 1, 5, 9 for tastes and
  beliefs; units 4, 8, 12 for community) offers an invented or public option marked the same (APPROACH "Sensitive
  ground"). Each section carries health's steps for "If a student tells you something"
  (docs/courses/health/APPROACH.md) on its teacher page.
- **Logic model (one page in the scope and sequence).** Inputs: two 40-minute sessions a week, a clear floor, step
  cards, adapted scenes, research and object cards, found and low-cost materials. Activities: the actor's tools,
  staging, design and devising taught in steps; plays, forms and companies studied with their setting; rehearsal with
  a critique and a revision; a showing each unit. Outputs: a portfolio folder of 12 pieces over the band with
  reflections, 12 showings, two marks a unit per student on the main look-for. Outcome claimed: met marks on the
  year's 24 ids and growth on the look-fors. Nothing here has been tried with students, and Curio Class makes no claim
  that arts study raises any other result (APPROACH "Sources").

