|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|
| 1 | 6 | Who is this character? *How do actors build a character from a scene's clues, and what do our own tastes and an artist's own life have to do with what we see?* | 18 | th-g6-actor; th-g6-notice | TH:Pr4.1.6b; TH:Pr5.1.6a; TH:Cr3.1.6b; TH:Cr1.1.6c; TH:Re7.1.6a; TH:Re8.1.6a; TH:Re8.1.6c | Performing (Pr4, Pr5); creating (Cr1, Cr3); responding (Re7, Re8); connecting touched through an artist's own life. Works: Charlie Chaplin, The Pawnshop (United States, 1916, a silent comedy short, public domain in the United States; the unit plan records its archive page), the clock scene, where body choices alone make a character; Chaplin built the Tramp from his London childhood and music-hall years (published biography); kyogen, the comic theater played between Noh plays in Japan since the fourteenth century (N�?gaku theatre, on UNESCO's list of intangible cultural heritage, 2008), with the kyogen actor Nomura Mansai (born 1966; the unit plan records whether living, as of its date), described and linked to UNESCO's page and a Japanese arts council's page, never copied; a Curio Class original two-character scene (two neighbors and a lost dog) the class performs | Retrieval in day 2's opener: grade 5's character built with body and voice and matched warm-ups (th-g5-actor) and the reaction backed by experience (th-g5-notice), each with its ten-minute reteach; the group agreements and critique protocol return as review on the first rehearsal day. TH:Cr1.1.6c: get to know a character by picturing its given circumstances (who, where, when, what just happened), on the character map. TH:Pr4.1.6b: try three body choices for one character (center of weight, pace, one gesture) and keep one. TH:Pr5.1.6a: see how the warm-up routine (body, breath, voice, focus) and one acting method (a "what if" from the given circumstances) apply to the scene, and pick the warm-up that fits it. TH:Cr3.1.6b: spot which body and voice traits work, in a partner's scene and in one's own, in the unit's words. TH:Re7.1.6a: write down and describe how the student reacted to the artists' decisions in a piece (an audience-journal entry naming one decision). TH:Re8.1.6a: explain how an artist's choice may come from the artist's own experience (Chaplin's walk and his London years, from a biography card Curio Class writes). TH:Re8.1.6c: recognize the student's own tastes and preferences by joining in and watching (a "what I enjoy and why" line; beliefs named only by the student's choice). SL.6.4 practiced, not owned (clear, ordered speaking on stage). Words: given circumstances, trait, warm-up, method, body, voice. Showing: the two-character scenes for the class. End-of-unit evidence (two check days), id by id: TH:Cr1.1.6c, the kept character map, scored after: four given-circumstances boxes, each tied to a line of the scene; TH:Pr4.1.6b, a guide look-for on the showing, marked live: a body choice that shows the character, held through the scene; TH:Pr5.1.6a, the kept rehearsal note: the warm-up chosen for the scene and one reason it fits; TH:Cr3.1.6b, a written item: two effective body or voice traits in the teacher's unseen scene, each with what it showed; TH:Re7.1.6a, a written item: an audience-journal entry on the unseen scene naming one decision and the student's reaction; TH:Re8.1.6a, a written item: one choice in an unseen description of an artist's work and how it may come from the artist's life, from its biography card; TH:Re8.1.6c, a written item: one thing the student enjoys in watching or acting, and why. Each item is marked met, partly or not yet. Out: a character's goal and inner thoughts (grade 7), tactics (grade 8). Tools: the character map, warm-up card, audience journal and scene cards, Curio Class's own; a stage-directions practice tool or its printed card. Free path: chairs, the voice, printed scenes. Once-a-week path: 9 sessions | First in grade 6 because every later unit needs the warm-up routine, the floor routine, the group agreements and a built character to stage; the written response opens the year so every later judgment grows from it (the warm-up-routine, character-map and audience-journal models are first used here). Longest unit: seven ids, the year's routines set up, and two grade 5 rows retrieved | Candidate: the bar's criterion 9 on set-up and close minutes when chairs move twice a session | Hazards and controls: movement on a shared floor (chairs and bags to the walls, room for arms out, soft soles or bare feet on a clean floor); no lifts, no falls, no contact; Chaplin's slapstick is described, never copied in contact; voice rest and no forced loud sound. No one plays a character by an accent or stereotype (th-g6-actor). TH:Re8.1.6a uses published facts of an artist's life, never the student's; TH:Re8.1.6c asks for tastes, and beliefs only if the student chooses, with an invented "a viewer who..." option marked the same. Kyogen: the sensitivity agent reads UNESCO's N�?gaku page and a Japanese arts council's kyogen page first; students study its walk and voice from the description, never imitate them as a joke or in costume, and the ritual piece Okina is not used. Health's steps if a student tells something. Agent reads: sensitivity; safety (hands-on). |
