rrel scene played with two goals, for the class. End-of-unit evidence (two check days), id by id: TH:Pr4.1.7b, a guide look-for on the showing: the scene played with two different goals, each readable; TH:Pr5.1.7a, the kept rehearsal note: two warm-ups or methods taken part in and what each did for the scene, with the teacher's rotating-list marks from the warm-up days; TH:Cr3.1.7b, a guide look-for on the showing: body and voice traits that fit the goal; TH:Cr1.1.7c, the kept character map: a six-line inner monologue of what the character thinks and wants; TH:Re7.1.7a, a written item: the student's entry on the teacher's unseen scene set beside a partner's, with one likeness and one difference; TH:Re8.1.7a, a written item: one choice in an unseen described work drawn from the artist's life, from its biography card; TH:Re8.1.7c, a written item: how the student's taste framed the pair's talk about the unseen scene. Each item is marked met, partly or not yet. Out: tactics against an obstacle (grade 8). Tools: character map, warm-up card, method cards, audience journal, Curio Class's own. Free path: chairs, the voice, printed scenes. Once-a-week path: 9 sessions | First in grade 7 because it reopens the floor and the routines after the summer and moves the character from circumstances to a goal and an inner life, which unit 6's staging and unit 8's woven roles use. Longest unit for the same reasons as unit 1 | Candidate: a third artist-from-life work by a maker from Asia or Africa, described and linked | A Raisin in the Sun: racism in housing is named plainly from the court case and published sources; no student reads a role from the play; the sensitivity agent reads the library's or museum's page and Hansberry's own published words first. Biography facts are public, never the student's own. Beliefs are named only by the student's choice, with an invented "a viewer who..." option marked the same. The Proposal's fainting is played seated, no falls. Hazards and controls 