| 6 | 7 | The lost ring. *How might a playwright have pictured his play's first staging, and which staging of a scene tells the story better?* | 14 | th-g7-imagine; th-g7-story | TH:Cr1.1.7a; TH:Cr2.1.7a; TH:Pr4.1.7a; TH:Cn11.2.7a; TH:Cn11.2.7b | Creating (Cr1, Cr2); performing (Pr4); connecting (Cn11). Works: Kalidasa, Shakuntala, or the Recognition of Shakuntala (India, in Sanskrit, about the fifth century CE; public domain English translations of 1855 and 1912; Curio Class retells the comic scene of the fisherman and the ring); the Natyashastra, the ancient Indian treatise on performance (about 200 BCE to 200 CE), for its stage shape and the stage manager's opening, described from a university's or a cultural body's page; a gold coin of the Gupta kings (India, fourth or fifth century CE, from the Metropolitan Museum of Art's open-access collection; the unit plan names the coin and its page) | Retrieval in day 2's opener: grade 6's staging fixes (th-g6-imagine, unit 2, days 19 to 32) and script markup and era research (th-g6-story, unit 2). TH:Pr4.1.7a: stage the ring scene two ways and weigh which strengthens the story (RL.7.3 practiced, not owned: how setting and characters shape the plot). TH:Cr1.1.7a: look at several viewpoints and fixes for a staging problem (the ring found in a fish must be seen by the audience; the fisherman, the guards and the audience each see it differently). TH:Cr2.1.7a: examine and defend the chosen staging in a short note using analysis, background and context (the Natyashastra's stage, a Gupta court). TH:Cn11.2.7a: research and talk about how Kalidasa may have pictured the first production (a court stage, a stage manager's opening, gesture for the chariot, no painted scenery). TH:Cn11.2.7b: study objects from the play's era and region (the coin, a ring) to explain a staging or design choice. Words: viewpoint, option, author, research, object, region. Showing: the ring scene in two stagings, for another class. End-of-unit evidence (two check days), id by id: TH:Pr4.1.7a, a guide look-for on the showing: both stagings tell the story clearly and the group names the stronger one; TH:Cr1.1.7a, the kept idea sheet: three fixes, each from a named viewpoint; TH:Cr2.1.7a, the kept defense note (five to eight sentences: claim, analysis, background); TH:Cn11.2.7a, a written item: from an unseen description of an ancient stage, two things its author may have pictured; TH:Cn11.2.7b, a written item: an unseen object card from another era and one staging or design choice it explains. Each item is marked met, partly or not yet. Out: pacing and two productions compared (grade 8). Tools: idea sheet, defense note frame, script markup, research and object cards, Curio Class's own. Free path: a card fish and ring, chairs, cloth. Once-a-week path: 7 sessions | After unit 5 because the stagings need characters with goals; before unit 7 because the design unit redesigns this scene's change of place. Research of the author's first production builds on grade 6's era cards | Candidate: a living Indian theater maker's staging of Shakuntala, described and linked, for a living voice | Shakuntala: the hermitage's sacred rites and the Natyashastra's opening worship are named and studied by reading only; no student performs a rite, a prayer or a hand gesture as costume, and no costume of another culture is worn; the curse is told plainly. The class performs only Curio Class's comic fisherman scene. The sensitivity agent reads a Sanskrit drama scholar's or a cultural body's page and the translation first. Hazards and controls: props of card; the guards' arrest is a spoken line, no grabbing or contact. Agent reads: sensitivity; safety (hands-on). |
