ng day; TH:Pr5.1.7b, the kept cue sheet: at least three elements chosen, each with its job; TH:Cr3.1.7c, the kept tech-rehearsal note: the elements weighed and one cut or changed, with why; TH:Re9.1.7b, a written item: how two elements of an unseen described production look and feel, and why; TH:Re9.1.7a, a written item: the student's preference in the teacher's unseen scene with evidence and a criterion; TH:Re9.1.7c, a written item: how the described production's aim draws in its audience. Each item is marked met, partly or not yet. Out: the real venue and simple technology (grade 8). Tools: cue sheet, design sketch sheet, criteria card, Curio Class's own; a cue-calling practice tool or its printed cue cards; a school device may play one sound cue. Free path: the room's light switch and blinds, found-object sound, cloth. Once-a-week path: 6 sessions | After unit 6 because the design solves that scene's change of place; preference grows from grade 6's judgment and needs unit 5's compared reactions. Before unit 8 because the woven piece uses another art and a designed cue | Candidate: a living designer from Africa or the Americas, described and linked, beside the two | Kabuki: the sensitivity agent reads UNESCO's page and a Japanese arts council's kabuki page first; kabuki makeup, costume and voice are never imitated; its all-male casting since the 1600s is named plainly as history. Hazards and controls as unit 3, and costume changes go over the student's own clothes only. Agent reads: sensitivity; safety (hands-on). |
