docs/courses/english-language-arts-grade-12/frame.md:8:- **Days.** 160 course days: 149 in units and 11 for course-level checks and respond days (three checks, each followed by respond days). A full year of 32 weeks, five lessons a week of about 50 minutes, with home reading of about 20 to 30 minutes on school nights while a long text is open (Hamlet across about five weeks; The Great Gatsby, about 48,000 words, in under four). Days follow load, not row count: unit 1 (15 days) starts seven year-long routines and a full process essay with a rewrite; unit 2 holds a whole play, three of the year's hardest reading rows and a process argument; unit 8 is the senior project of about seven weeks (36 days), which shares its question with US History II's capstone (history chooses it on its day 123, this course on its day 125); unit 5 is short because it reads two essays in excerpt (Du Bois chapters 1 and 3, Woolf chapters 1 and 3) for three structural rows. Each unit includes an end-of-unit assessment and response time once. All assumed ELA rows were used in grade 11, so they return through retrieval inside the first relevant teaching day. By grade number, last use in grade g means retrieval in g+1 and a readiness check in g+2 or later; an older row's check precedes the unit, except unit 1's last ten minutes of day 1 after its hook. Unit 8 also has a mid-unit assessment.
docs/courses/english-language-arts-grade-12/frame.md:10:- **Base text.** None. ELA 6 to 12 is Curio Class's own (docs/sources/BASE-TEXTS.md, "Our own"). Every anchor is public domain in the United States (published 1930 or earlier), a US government work (court opinions, presidential addresses, government reports), or a Curio Class original; each selected edition's date and rights must be checked in its text record before unit planning; this pass records the blank checks rather than claiming they are complete. No commercial text collection is used. Under Cory's 2026-11-13 rule, anchors are picked for being recognizable and as recent as the license allows: The Great Gatsby (1925), A Room of One's Own (1929), Hughes, McKay and Cullen, and US government works through the present (Tinker, 1969; Reagan's Challenger address, 1986; Mahanoy, 2021; the most recent inaugural address). The optional recent-memoir and recent-speech quotations were dropped in the scope and sequence, zero days; the current government-work address remains a required comparison. No student is sent to a library or a paid copy: every required anchor must have a checked print copy in the Curio Class library before its unit is planned, and the course text record lists which are on file. Period texts that hold slurs are printed with each slur left out, an omission mark and a short editor's note; the edition is otherwise complete and every omission is disclosed (BASELINE rule 6).
docs/courses/english-language-arts-grade-12/frame.md:11:- **What this year anchors on.** Hamlet and The Great Gatsby read whole; The Importance of Being Earnest read whole as the comic pair to Hamlet; the court opinions in excerpt and Federalist 10 and 51 whole. The literary anchors also include Pride and Prejudice chapters 1 to 3, Prufrock, and the Hughes/McKay/Cullen three-poem Harlem Renaissance cluster. Kennedy and Brown remain required paired comparisons, with their full assigned reading and reasoning work retained. The independent long book: Pride and Prejudice (1813) is offered first, carrying on from its opening chapters in unit 3; the shelf adds The Age of Innocence (1920, on this shelf only), A Farewell to Arms (1929), The Sun Also Rises (1926), Mrs Dalloway (1925), Passing (1929), Jane Eyre (1847), Great Expectations (1861), and Mourning Dove's Cogewea, the Half-Blood (1927), all public domain in the United States, with log and conference (e12-reading-band). Frankenstein is not offered: grade 10 reads it whole.
docs/courses/english-language-arts-grade-12/frame.md:12:- **Who wrote the texts.** British and American writers across four centuries, Black and white, men and women; unit 4's period choice set adds a Japanese American memoir (Etsu Inagaki Sugimoto, A Daughter of the Samurai, 1925), a Mexican American story (María Cristina Mena, 'The Gold Vanity Set', 1913) and a Native novel (Cogewea, also on the shelf). Grade 11 reads Occom, Apess and Sui Sin Far, so no author repeats across the two years.
docs/courses/english-language-arts-grade-12/frame.md:14:- **Anchor text records.** The course text record is filed in the scope and sequence, using data/plans/ela-texts.csv and distinguishing recorded status from verified edition, rights, measure and timing. The fixed rule counts anchors only, one poem cluster or paired set read as an anchor once, and excludes paired/short reads: 18 anchors, 11 informational and 7 literary, 61.1 percent informational. This replaces the old 13 of 17 (76.5 percent) catalogue count. Kennedy and Brown become required paired comparisons; Austen's opening, the three-poem Harlem cluster and Prufrock become anchors with sustained return readings. The shared inventory changes await the structure pass. Writing is counted by the principal purpose of five process products: three arguments (Hamlet, mock opinion, senior research claim), one explanation and one narrative, 60/20/20 by product count. The senior paper's informative sections do not make it a second informative product. The three cold essays are argument, argument/analysis and informative, separately counted. These descriptive shares do not change assignments.
docs/courses/english-language-arts-grade-12/frame.md:15:- **Routines all year** (each starts in unit 1 and runs in every unit): the daily exit ticket and the weekly one-sitting analytic paragraph, with three one-sitting essays on cold texts that draw on a literary and an informational text at once (W.11-12.9a and W.11-12.9b together, e12-routine-writing); a seminar at least twice a unit, one of them led by students who set its goal, deadline and roles (e12-seminar); about 8 to 10 words a week from the unit's texts, met before, during and after reading, plus the student's own word log from grade 9 (e12-academic-words; the scope and sequence sets each list); three minutes weekly on six new derivational families (reflect, discern, exclude, deliberate, justify, qualify), with prior-grade forms retrieved for two minutes in the same weekly block and later families revisited, L.11-12.4b; the independent book (e12-reading-band); the long-sentence routine from grade 11 on one anchor sentence a week, now led by students, the year's own texts (Woolf, Du Bois, Fitzgerald) as models in place of a paid style book (review of grade 11's e11-syntax-effect until unit 2 takes it up). No timer is shown to students; the teacher keeps time.
docs/courses/english-language-arts-grade-12/frame.md:30:| e11-process (W.11-12.4 to 6): process taught in full, a piece updated once | Unit 1, e12-process | The student writes the revision plan, tries a new approach, keeps a portfolio |
docs/courses/english-language-arts-grade-12/frame.md:32:| e11-routine-writing (W.11-12.9, W.11-12.10): the weekly paragraph, three cold essays | Unit 1, e12-routine-writing | Cold essays on a literary and an informational text at once |
docs/courses/english-language-arts-grade-12/frame.md:53:| e11-informative (W.11-12.2): a literary movement from texts read | Unit 6, e12-informative | A concept from another course for a general reader, own sources, technical words managed |
docs/courses/english-language-arts-grade-12/frame.md:62:Grade 12 is the last course: every thread ends at the band's end, the College and Career Readiness anchor standard the code belongs to. The end of each thread is checked in the course-level checks or the senior project, by a task the student does alone.
docs/courses/english-language-arts-grade-12/frame.md:80:| Writing from texts and routinely (e12-routine-writing, unit 1) | CCRA.W.9, W.10 | The three cold essays on two texts at once |
docs/courses/english-language-arts-grade-12/frame.md:111:Unit order: the narrative first, while seniors write application essays; the year's hardest literary reading (Hamlet) early, while there is time for it, with its comic pair after; the 1920s once US History II has taught the decade; essays before speeches, speeches and a policy question before the law; the research project last, sharing its question with US History II's capstone. Day ranges follow the current scope and sequence.
docs/courses/english-language-arts-grade-12/frame.md:115:| 1 | **Telling your own story: reflective writing, 1883 to 1916** (about days 1 to 15). How do Twain, Addams and Frost turn one moment into a claim about who they became? | 15 | e12-narrative; e12-process; e12-usage-conventions; e12-routine-writing; e12-seminar; e12-reading-band; e12-academic-words | W.11-12.3; W.11-12.4; W.11-12.5; W.11-12.6; L.11-12.1; L.11-12.2; W.11-12.9; W.11-12.10; SL.11-12.1; RL.11-12.10; RI.11-12.10; L.11-12.6 | Not used (ELA) | Models read as craft: Mark Twain's 'Two Views of the Mississippi' (Life on the Mississippi, chapter 9, 1883: what learning the river cost him), Jane Addams's Twenty Years at Hull-House, chapter 4 (1910: the years of drift after college and the moment she chose her work), Frost's 'The Road Not Taken' (1916) with the misreading it often gets, and a Curio Class model essay; candidate: a short quoted passage of a well-known recent memoir inside the lesson. The reflective narrative (process, about 650 to 1,000 words, the length of an application essay): W.11-12.3a to e (a problem and its significance set out, one or more points of view, a smooth sequence that builds toward a particular tone and outcome, telling detail, a reflective close). The process with the student leading (W.11-12.4 to 6): the student's own revision plan, a conference, one draft rewritten with a new approach; published to a class page in a shared-device session posted by the teacher, no student account, and updated after feedback (W.11-12.6; paper path: a posted copy and the binder, the technology part then recorded unfinished). Contested usage met in the drafts (singular 'they', 'data is', the split infinitive) settled with Fowler's 1926 usage guide beside a free current dictionary's usage notes and defended in a note (L.11-12.1a and 1b); hyphenation and spelling in editing (L.11-12.2a, L.11-12.2b). Year routines started: the exit ticket and weekly paragraph, the first two-text paragraph (Frost's poem and Addams's chapter, W.11-12.9a, W.11-12.9b); seminars led by students who set the goal, deadline and roles (SL.11-12.1a to d), with a partner talk one-on-one before each; the independent book chosen, Pride and Prejudice offered first; the unit word list and the student's own log. | Seniors write application essays in the fall: the course's first piece serves that use (a student may submit their own). Short texts let the year's routines start before Hamlet. Opens with an unmarked retrieval task on grade 11's two-viewpoint narrative and process routine (e11-narrative, e11-process). | Candidate: settled in the scope and sequence. Dropped: further reflective model and recent-memoir quotation, zero days; three anchors and original model suffice. | Students may write about hard experiences: they choose what to share, the teacher reads privately, and no student is asked to disclose; a disclosure goes to the school's support process. Keller and Hurston are grade 9's anchors and are not reread here. Sensitivity read, after the counselling and trauma guidance the brief names, for the writing task. |
docs/courses/english-language-arts-grade-12/frame.md:116:| 2 | **Hamlet: what can be known, and what can be done** (about days 16 to 38). What does Hamlet know for certain, and how does not knowing shape what he does? | 23 | e12-evidence-uncertain; e12-lit-themes; e12-lit-word-tone; e12-lit-interpretations; e12-word-strategies; e12-syntax-effect; e12-argument | RL.11-12.1; RI.11-12.1; RL.11-12.2; RL.11-12.4; RL.11-12.7; L.11-12.4; L.11-12.3; W.11-12.1 | Not used (ELA) | Hamlet (about 1600) read whole across about five weeks, acts read aloud in class with the public-domain volunteer full-cast audio recording beside the text; Early Modern words checked in Onions's A Shakespeare Glossary (1911) and Schmidt's Shakespeare Lexicon. Evidence and uncertainty found by students (is the Ghost honest? is the madness feigned?), the two best readings argued with evidence; the RI part on literary nonfiction: Coleridge's 1818 lecture on Hamlet in excerpt, what it claims and leaves uncertain. Two or more themes chosen and traced without a tracker (knowing and acting; corruption; mortality), how they interact, an objective summary (RL.11-12.2). The soliloquies and puns ('a little more than kin, and less than kind'): figurative and connotative meanings, words with more than one meaning, language that is fresh or beautiful, the Early Modern meaning checked first (RL.11-12.4, the Shakespeare part). Interpretations: three versions of the same scenes (the 1921 silent film, the public-domain full-cast audio recording, and a live class staging of one scene), each judged against the text; the American dramatist part, taught in grade 11 on Trifles, retrieved in one lesson that sets a Trifles scene's two versions beside Hamlet's, the lesson supplying the scene and both versions so a student new to Curio Class needs nothing from grade 11 (RL.11-12.7 in full). Early Modern words ('quietus', 'bodkin', 'conscience' in its older sense) by context, word changes, the historical dictionary and verification (L.11-12.4a to d). Shakespeare's inverted syntax read for sense, and a paragraph of the student's own revised for one effect (L.11-12.3a). The literary argument (process, about 1,500 words): W.11-12.1a to e, claims and counterclaims developed fairly and thoroughly, the strengths and limitations of each for a named audience, the norms of literary criticism; an argument map before drafting; one one-on-one conference with the teacher on the draft. Discussion in the settings SL.11-12.1 names: a teacher-led discussion of the closet scene beside the student-led seminars. | The year's hardest literary reading comes early, while the fall has time for it and before units 3 and 4 build on it (the comic pair, the novel). The evidence, theme and word rows need a long text read whole; Hamlet is the standard's own example. Opens with an unmarked retrieval task on grade 11's evidence and theme work (e11-evidence-uncertain, e11-lit-themes). | Candidate: settled in the scope and sequence. Kept: Ophelia songs on 27 inside the whole play, zero added days; second Shakespeare soliloquy with context on existing assessment 35, one existing day. | Suicide is discussed (the soliloquy, Ophelia's death), with murder and grief: a content note first, the school's support contacts given, no student asked to argue for or against self-harm or to write in a character's voice about it. Gertrude and Ophelia read with attention to how the play treats women. Sensitivity read, after the safe-messaging guidance the brief names. |
docs/courses/english-language-arts-grade-12/frame.md:117:| 3 | **Money, marriage and class: Swift, Austen and Wilde, 1729 to 1895** (about days 39 to 50). What do these writers show about how money decides who marries whom, and how does laughing at it make the point? | 12 | e12-lit-irony; e12-lit-structure; e12-figurative-nuance | RL.11-12.6; RL.11-12.5; L.11-12.5 | Not used (ELA) | Swift's 'A Modest Proposal' (1729, about 3,400 words): sustained satire whose persona never breaks, students prove Swift's real view from the text alone; the opening chapters of Pride and Prejudice (1813) read at home, with one class day on irony in the narrator; Wilde's The Importance of Being Earnest (1895, three acts) read whole, its wit; Hamlet's sarcasm and dramatic irony from unit 2 returned (RL.11-12.6). Structure: Hamlet's tragic resolution set beside Earnest's comic one (the found handbag), where to begin and end and what each ending does to the whole; Gatsby's opening and closing pages, looked at again in unit 4 (RL.11-12.5, the standard's own example pair). Figures in context and their role: Hamlet's paradoxes ('I must be cruel only to be kind'), Wilde's epigrams, Swift's understatement (L.11-12.5a); near-synonyms in Swift and Austen ('prudent', 'sensible', 'amiable') and their nuance (L.11-12.5b). A one-sitting essay: how Swift's persona gives him away. | After the tragedy, its comic pair, so the two resolutions are compared while Hamlet is fresh. Swift sharpens grade 11's short satire into the sustained form, and Austen opens the shelf's first book. Opens with an unmarked retrieval task on grade 11's stated-against-meant chart (e11-lit-irony). | Candidate: settled in the scope and sequence. Dropped: further Wilde essay, zero days; whole comedy and comparison fill the unit. | 'A Modest Proposal' proposes eating poor children to condemn the treatment of the Irish poor: set up satire and the Irish famine and poverty context before the first read (the row's note); no student is asked to write a satire about a real group's suffering. Earnest's jokes about class and marriage read in their time. Sensitivity read, after the study of Irish historians' accounts the brief names. |
docs/courses/english-language-arts-grade-12/frame.md:118:| 4 | **The 1920s: the American dream and its price** (about days 54 to 71). What does the American dream cost the people who chase it in these 1920s texts, and who pays? | 18 | e12-lit-author-choices; e12-lit-american-modern | RL.11-12.3; RL.11-12.9 | Not used (ELA) | The Great Gatsby (1925, about 48,000 words, a reading level of about 1070 but high qualitative demand from its unreliable narrator) read whole: the author's choices about setting (East and West Egg, the valley of ashes), the order of action (Gatsby's past held back and told late) and how characters are introduced and developed, each choice's impact and what a different choice would lose (RL.11-12.3). The Harlem Renaissance beside it: Hughes's 'I, Too' (1926, answering Whitman, read in grade 11), McKay's 'If We Must Die' (1919), Cullen's 'Yet Do I Marvel' (1925), Jean Toomer's Cane (1923), the complete November Cotton Flower section; Eliot's 'The Love Song of J. Alfred Prufrock' (1915) for the decade's modernism. American literature of the early twentieth century: students choose two texts of the period that treat a similar theme (the dream and what it costs) and compare them, from the unit's texts and a choice set: Etsu Inagaki Sugimoto's A Daughter of the Samurai (1925, the chapters on her life in America), María Cristina Mena's 'The Gold Vanity Set' (1913), and chapter 1 of Mourning Dove's Cogewea, the Half-Blood (1927); then look back, setting one 1920s text against a grade 11 text on the same theme (Gatsby's self-making against 'Self-Reliance' or Douglass), which completes RL.11-12.9 across the three centuries. A one-sitting comparison of the student's pair. | After US History II's 1920s unit (its days 13 to 25), so the decade is known. After units 2 and 3 read whole plays, a whole novel; Gatsby's structure was met in unit 3. Opens with an unmarked retrieval task on grade 11's period comparison (e11-lit-american-periods). | Candidate: settled in the scope and sequence. Dropped: Winter Dreams, zero days; whole novel, Prufrock and Harlem cluster plus choice set fill the unit. | 'If We Must Die' answers the racial violence of 1919: give the setting before reading (the row's note). Tom Buchanan's racist speech about a book of 'scientific' racism: read as Fitzgerald's portrait of him, stated plainly, never as true. Myrtle's death and Gatsby's are violent. The novel's antisemitic portrait of Meyer Wolfsheim (chapters 4 and 9) named and read as evidence of the narrator's view; the unit falls after US History II's Holocaust unit (its days 42 to 56) for the chapters that carry it, which come after about day 57 here, and both courses coordinate content-note wording at unit planning. Sensitivity read, after the Black-led and Jewish-led study the brief names, and the choice set's own study. |
docs/courses/english-language-arts-grade-12/frame.md:120:| 6 | **Public words and a public decision, 1933 to today** (about days 87 to 102). What makes a speech move its listeners, and should our town change its zoning to bring rents down? | 16 | e12-info-rhetoric; e12-info-multiple-sources; e12-informative | RI.11-12.6; SL.11-12.3; RI.11-12.7; SL.11-12.2; W.11-12.2 | Not used (ELA) | Twentieth-century addresses read and heard in the speaker's own public-domain recording, chosen for rhetoric that is particularly effective: Franklin Roosevelt's First Inaugural (1933), Kennedy's Inaugural (1961) and Reagan's address on the Challenger disaster (1986); the most recent presidential inaugural address (a US government work) is read as a current speech with the year's texts kept balanced, not as an exemplar; point of view and purpose where the rhetoric is particularly effective, how style and content give each its power (RI.11-12.6); the speaker's stance, premises, links among ideas, word choice, emphasis and tone, and what delivery (pause, emphasis) adds; for one recorded address, students name a claim that matters, tell its evidence apart from assertion or appeal, judge whether the reasoning and evidence support the claim, and say how delivery strengthens or hides that support, a performance the unit's assessment brief names (SL.11-12.3 in full). An informal two-minute talk, a reaction to one speech, beside the formal policy decision (SL.11-12.4's informal tasks, practiced here; the row sits in unit 8). A decision: should our town change its zoning to bring rents down? from a set the students partly build, all free: a Census Bureau housing data release, a HUD or GAO report, a city council's public minutes and a recorded public hearing (paper path: a transcript), and two short original Curio Class pieces written as a news report and an op-ed; each source's credibility judged, discrepancies among the data named, a decision reached and defended (RI.11-12.7, SL.11-12.2). Candidate: a short quoted passage of a well-known recent speech inside the lesson. The informative piece (process, about 1,500 words): explain a complex idea from another course to a general reader (supply and demand behind the rent question, or a science concept such as how vaccines train the immune system), with headings, a figure or table and an analogy, technical words managed, sources the student chooses, and an end on implications (W.11-12.2a to f). | Speeches after the essays, and the policy decision right after them because both judge a speaker's premises. Before unit 7, which asks for reasoning in the law and needs the source judgment. The informative piece borrows an idea from Economics, which US History II's unit 1 already opened (its days 1 to 12). | Candidate: settled in the scope and sequence. Dropped: transit alternative and recent-speech quotation, zero days; one shared housing set and current inaugural suffice. | Housing costs may touch students' own families: no student is asked to share their housing situation; the question is argued as a city's choice. The current speech is chosen for its rhetoric, not its party; the teacher keeps the year's texts balanced on current politics. |
docs/courses/english-language-arts-grade-12/frame.md:122:| 8 | **The senior research project** (about days 125 to 160). What question do you need answered, and what do the sources say? (each student's own question) | 36 | e12-research; e12-presentation | W.11-12.7; W.11-12.8; SL.11-12.4; SL.11-12.5; SL.11-12.6 | Not used (ELA) | A short project first (two days, three sources) on the context of a text read this year, to retrieve the cycle. Then the senior project, about seven weeks: the student's own question (in a school paired with US History II, a question from 1920 to today), narrowed or broadened with the change and its reason documented; at least eight sources, at least three of them primary, from authoritative print and digital collections in open archives, found with advanced searches; each source's strengths and limitations for task, purpose and audience; an annotated source list; information integrated selectively, plagiarism and overreliance on one source avoided, cited to the Curio Class style sheet (W.11-12.7, W.11-12.8). An essay of about 2,500 words. A talk of about ten minutes with questions, with visuals chosen to show evidence, given twice: to informed rotating student panels, then adapted to different listeners unfamiliar with the inquiry (a younger class where school allows); the teacher is lead judge and another class or staff may attend where school allows (SL.11-12.4 to 6; digital media used strategically on a shared device, the paper published and updated with the talk's media, the end of W.11-12.6; a printed handout as the paper path, the digital parts then recorded unfinished). Milestones, one calendar with US History II: question chosen about day 125 (history's day 123); annotated source list of eight, at least three primary, about day 135 (history unit 10's approximate seam, with a provisional list available 134 and the complete list 135), one list serving both courses; full draft about day 145; presentation taught and rehearsed once by day 147, before history's unit 12 opens on its day 148; talks days 150 to 156 in two forms for two audiences, serving history's unit 12 where its beyond-classroom audience requirement is also supplied by another class or staff; an internal panel alone leaves that history-specific part unfinished; final paper about day 158. Cut order if seniors leave early: the paper's revision days go first, then, where the course runs alone, the second form of the talk; the missing contrasting-audience evidence then remains unfinished. Where paired with US History II both forms stay, since history scores two forms for two audiences (D4.3). The scope and sequence's ten-fold shorter path keeps both forms; the source list and the draft are never cut. The students' own word list for the project (L.11-12.6, review). The portfolio closes the year: the five process pieces, one rewritten with a new approach. | Last, so the whole year is open to it and it is the band's capstone. Shares its question with US History II's capstone where a school teaches both (the shared brief below): ELA owns the process, citation, essay length and talk. Opens with an unmarked retrieval task on grade 11's research cycle (e11-research). | Candidate: settled in the scope and sequence. Dropped: externally framed research-question pattern, zero days; the owned question is self-generated and revised by evidence. | The student's question may be hard history or a hard personal topic: the teacher screens each question and source set with the content notes of the matching unit; students choose what to share in talks. Student panels rotate, with the teacher as lead judge; another class or staff attends only where school allows. No outside adult panel or extra adult observer is required. |
docs/courses/english-language-arts-grade-12/frame.md:126:Each check uses new texts on earlier units' content and a form absent from the last two weeks. It lists only newly sampled codes, reuses recent records without rescoring, holds one two-text essay, and has respond time once. A code taught later is never claimed early. Individual evidence inside ten days is collected once. The scope and sequence gives the 50-, 40- and 35-minute routes for 30 students.
docs/courses/english-language-arts-grade-12/frame.md:130:| Check 1 | 3 | 2 | 1 | Days 51 to 53, week 11: new literary and literary nonfiction pair on earlier unit 1/2 content. Day 51 samples RL.1/2/4/10 and RI.1/10, including uncertainty, interacting themes/summary and figurative/connotative/multiple meanings, fresh language and tone. Reuse days 48/49 RL.5/6 records without rescoring. Day 52 argument on both texts, all W.1a to e and W.9a/b with conventions; first independent checkpoint, access supports separate. One respond day. |
docs/courses/english-language-arts-grade-12/frame.md:131:| Check 2 | 5 | 3 | 1 | Days 83 to 86, week 17: new literary nonfiction/poem pair on public creativity from units 1 to 4. Reuse day 80 RI.2/3 and day 81 RI.5 records. New evidence: RI.1/10, RL.3/6/10, L.5a/b including the figure's role and near-synonym nuance. Day 84 argument/analysis, all W.1a to e, W.9a/b and L.3a, a form taught in unit 2; informative writing moves to check 3. Day 85 student-led seminar; reuse existing conference setting record, no new one-to-one collection. One respond day. |
docs/courses/english-language-arts-grade-12/frame.md:132:| Check 3 | 7 | 2 | 2 | Days 121 to 124, week 25: bounded new high-band literary/literary nonfiction read and two interpretations, newly sampling RL.7/10 and RI.10 only. Reuse day 117 RI.4 key-term record; evidence/themes/words and speech evaluation use earlier full records. Day 122 informative essay on both texts, W.2a to f after unit 6 instruction, W.9a/b and L.3a; reuse day 119 conventions, never rescore it. Narrative record remains days 13/14. No task form from the last two weeks is repeated. Two respond days, then the research question opens unit 8. |
docs/courses/english-language-arts-grade-12/frame.md:134:Check and respond days: 11. Days in units: 149. Total: 160.
docs/courses/english-language-arts-grade-12/frame.md:145:| e12-research, unit 8, about days 125 to 160 | us2-research-paper (the capstone researched argument) | Question chosen on unit 9's respond day (about day 123); units 10 and 11 add sources (one of them arguing against the claim, so the day 145 draft answers it); unit 12 days 148 to 160 | The shared brief: one question (1920 to today) and one source set (eight sources here, at least three primary, which meets history's six with three primary), chosen at check 3's respond day here and unit 9's respond day there; milestones as in unit 8 (source list about day 135, draft about day 145, presentation taught and rehearsed by day 147, talks days 150 to 156 in two forms for two audiences serving history's unit 12, final paper about day 158); history owns the historical question and the counterargument, ELA the process, citation, the 2,500-word essay and the talk; in a short senior year the paired talk keeps both forms, and history keeps both critiques and the action (US History II scope and sequence, unit 12). If not paired: this unit teaches the whole cycle alone |
docs/courses/english-language-arts-grade-12/frame.md:156:| e12-informative, unit 6 | econ-fiscal-monetary (an economic idea for a general reader) | Economics unit 6, its days 45 to 51 of the spring half (about year days 125 to 131) | After this unit when Economics runs in the spring. If later: the piece explains supply and demand, which US History II's unit 1 taught first (its days 1 to 12), or a science concept the student has studied; ELA teaches only the explaining, never new economics |
docs/courses/english-language-arts-grade-12/frame.md:177:1. **Days.** Settled 2026-11-14 (Cory, the time a day as picked): 160 (149 in units, 11 check and respond days), five 50-minute lessons a week, with home reading while the long texts are open. Unit 1 has 15 days for its routines and full process essay; the research project takes 36 days (about seven weeks) and opens about day 125, on the question chosen with US History II at its day 123. Scope question for Cory only if a school's senior ELA is a semester.
docs/courses/english-language-arts-grade-12/frame.md:181:5. **Recent texts (Cory's 2026-11-13 rule).** The government-work and 1920s selections remain, with anchor/paired roles in the course text record. The scope and sequence drops the optional recent-memoir quotation in unit 1 and recent-speech quotation in unit 6, zero days, because the required models and current inaugural already fill those roles.
